Do not start the chat agent host on Android.

The agent host is a separate server process that runs chat sessions through
harnesses of its own, and on this server it registers one: the Copilot SDK
harness, which spawns a second process for its runtime. Neither is needed for
chat. The chat view and its default local harness come from the built-in
Copilot extension and run in the extension host; the agent-host harness is
used only when chosen, since chat.defaultToCopilotHarness and
chat.editor.preferCopilotHarness both default to false. On 1.138 the pair
still started with every window, signed in or not, and held two phantom
process slots and about 330 MB. On 1.139 the harness looks for its runtime
in a @github/copilot-sdk-<platform>-<arch> package and none that can run here
is in the tree, so the backend would never start while the host still did,
rethrowing the resolve error into session listing.

What starts the host, read from 1.139.1. server-main.js is launched without
--agent-host-* flags, so setupServerServices (serverServices.ts) takes its
default branch: a ServerAgentHostManager in 'lazy' mode, which forks nothing
on construction, behind an AgentHostChannel registered as 'agentHostProxy'.
The only caller of ensureStarted() there is that channel's endpoint
resolver, run when a renderer calls 'connect' on it. The only renderer code
that calls 'connect' is EditorRemoteAgentHostServiceClient._connect(),
reached only from startAgentHost(), which the client calls from an autorun
while IAgentHostEnablementService.enabled is true. In the web workbench
(workbench.web.main.ts, and sessions.web.main.ts likewise) that service is
WebAgentHostEnablementService, whose runtime flag is
!!environmentService.remoteAuthority, true for every window this server
serves.

So the cut is that flag. With it false, enabled is false from construction,
the autorun never connects, the server never forks, and the Copilot backend,
which only the host spawns, never starts either. The agentHostEnabled
context key is then false, so the agent-host session types, pickers and the
Host Settings command are hidden and new chats take the local harness. The
client object still exists and never connects; every caller that could send
it a request is gated on that key or starts from a user action. The two
harness settings, chat.defaultToCopilotHarness and
chat.editor.preferCopilotHarness, stay visible in Settings and do nothing.
Cutting on the server instead, by registering UnavailableAgentHostChannel,
would stop the fork but leave all of that UI enabled and failing against a
channel that refuses to connect.

It is decided here, synchronously, rather than read from the server, because
the remote environment arrives after this constructor runs and the autorun
fires on the first value it sees.

isAndroid is the user-agent test, not patch 0001's isAndroidPlatform, which
reads process.platform and is false in any browser. 0001 warns that the two
answer different questions. Here they coincide: this tree only runs on
Android, and its workbench is only ever shown by the app's WebView on the
same device. A desktop browser reaching the server some other way would
still start the host, and get the host process without a backend.

The log line is also the only text in the hunk that survives minification,
so it is what the Verify stage greps workbench.js for. The injected
ILogService is one upstream's own unit test for this class does not supply;
no gate here runs that test.

